{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction of the building is completed. Rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings, exempt from local rent control ordinances for 30 years following completion of construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built_within_years","role":"exempt","years":30,"basis":"construction"},{"type":"other","text":"rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building."]},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years after completion of construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Municipalities may pass rent control or rent leveling ordinances; this law exempts newly constructed multiple dwellings from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act limits on rent increases","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent. A rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; the notice to quit and notice of rent increase are required before an owner can evict a tenant for nonpayment of an increased rent. Increases must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a landlord seeks to increase the rent or to evict for nonpayment of an increased rent.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"The increase in rent must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase prohibition for conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium or cooperative ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion. Increases for new services or amenities are still allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all tenants in a building converted to condominium or cooperative form of ownership or to fee simple ownership of units, regardless of eligibility for protected tenancy.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies to tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a dwelling unit used as a residence only by the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; the Anti-Eviction Act applies to written and oral leases. A Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except eviction for nonpayment of rent.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"A Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except an eviction for nonpayment of rent.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duties and withholding penalties","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving notices, paying interest and returning it.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental security deposits, including deposits made by or on behalf of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance such as welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"The civil penalty applies where the tenant has received financial assistance through a State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds the security deposit of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense; unlawfully using security deposit money may be charged as a disorderly persons offense with a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source of lawful income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing, including on the basis of the applicant's source of lawful income, and must treat applicants equally in the rental of housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex and age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"two-family owner occupied dwellings","unit_limit":2},{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home","unit_limit":null}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law applies to all landlord -tenant relationships, except those involving two -family owner occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident -occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, i.e. YMCA and age -restricted housing, as it pertains to familial status (N.J.S.A. 10:5-5(n))."]},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, such as the YMCA, and age-restricted housing as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to fines and penalties of up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
